3.12 for environment

git clone https://github.com/Amitmakode/My_CRM.git
cd My_CRM

then install pythoin version 3.11 and 3.12
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y

Install Required Dependencies
These packages are required to build Python.
sudo apt install -y build-essential checkinstall
sudo apt install -y libreadline-dev libncursesw5-dev libssl-dev \
libsqlite3-dev tk-dev libgdbm-dev libc6-dev libbz2-dev \
libffi-dev zlib1g-dev wget

download python
cd /usr/src
sudo wget https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.11.9/Python-3.11.9.tgz
sudo tar xzf Python-3.11.9.tgz

Compile Python
cd Python-3.11.9
sudo ./configure --enable-optimizations
sudo make altinstall

verify installation
python3.11 --version

Install pip for Python 3.11
python3.11 -m ensurepip
upgrade pip: 
python3.11 -m pip install --upgrade pip

Create Virtual Environment (Recommended for Django)
Inside your project:
python3.11 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
check
python --version

Install Project Requirements
pip install -r requirements.txt

Check Gunicorn Path
Since you installed it inside virtual environment, check its location.
Run:
which gunicorn
correct path:- /root/My_CRM/venv/bin/gunicorn

Edit Gunicorn Service File
Open service file:
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/gunicorn.service

correct configuration:

[Unit]
Description=gunicorn daemon
After=network.target

[Service]
User=root
Group=www-data
WorkingDirectory=/root/My_CRM

ExecStart=/root/My_CRM/venv/bin/gunicorn \
          --workers 3 \
          --bind unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock \
          crm.wsgi:application

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

Reload Systemd
After saving:
sudo systemctl daemon-reload

Restart Gunicorn
sudo systemctl restart gunicorn

Check Status
sudo systemctl status gunicorn
correct you see active: active(running)

If Still Not Working
Check logs:
journalctl -u gunicorn  or   journalctl -u gunicorn -n 50 --no-pager

## Another Common Issue
Sometimes people install Gunicorn in a virtual environment but run systemd as another user, so it cannot access the path.
If that happens change:
User=root
or
User=ubuntu
depending on your server user.

Quick Check (Important)
Run this manually first:
/root/My_CRM/venv/bin/gunicorn --bind 0.0.0.0:8000 crm.wsgi:application
If this command runs, Gunicorn is installed correctly.

Your Gunicorn service is now running successfully.
Your status shows
running or active then configure Nginx

Install Nginx
sudo apt update
sudo apt install nginx -y

Start it:
sudo systemctl start nginx
sudo systemctl enable nginx

Check:
sudo systemctl status nginx

Create Nginx Config
sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/mycrm
Paste this:

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name YOUR_SERVER_IP;

    location = /favicon.ico { access_log off; log_not_found off; }

    location /static/ {
        root /root/My_CRM;
    }

    location / {
        include proxy_params;
        proxy_pass http://unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock;
    }
}


Enable the Site
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/mycrm /etc/nginx/sites-enabled

Test Configuration
sudo nginx -t

If successful you will see:
syntax is ok
test is successful

Restart Nginx
sudo systemctl restart nginx

Open in Browser
http://YOUR-EC2-PUBLIC-IP
Your Django CRM project should load

iff any error comes then 
ls /root/My_CRM/
gunicorn.sock

Test Gunicorn Socket Manually
Run:
curl --unix-socket /root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock localhost
If Django is working you should see HTML response.

Check Nginx Configuration

Open config:

sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/mycrm

Make sure it looks like this:

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name YOUR_PUBLIC_IP;

    location / {
        include proxy_params;
        proxy_pass http://unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock;
    }

    location /static/ {
        root /root/My_CRM;
    }
}
Test Nginx Config
sudo nginx -t

Then restart:
sudo systemctl restart nginx
 Check Nginx Error Logs (Very Important)

Run:

sudo tail -50 /var/log/nginx/error.log

This will show the real reason of 502.

Check Gunicorn Logs
journalctl -u gunicorn -n 50

Fix Permission Issue (Very Common)
Because your project is under /root, Nginx may not access the socket.
Run:
sudo chmod 755 /root
sudo chmod -R 755 /root/My_CRM

Restart services:
sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
sudo systemctl restart nginx

Test Gunicorn via Port (Debug)
Run:
/root/My_CRM/venv/bin/gunicorn --bind 0.0.0.0:8000 crm.wsgi:application

Then open:
http://YOUR-IP:8000
If it loads, Django and Gunicorn are fine.

Because your project path is:
/root/My_CRM

Nginx cannot access /root directory, causing 502.

Professional Fix 
Move project to:
/home/ubuntu/My_CRM     or       /var/www/My_CRM

This is the standard production location.
causing the 502 error. 🚀
sbow you like that 
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <title>Bad Request (400)</title>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Bad Request (400)</h1><p></p>
</body>
</html>

But Django is rejecting the request because of ALLOWED_HOSTS.
Fix: Update ALLOWED_HOSTS
Open Django settings:
nano crm/settings.py
Find this line:
ALLOWED_HOSTS = []
Change it to your EC2 public IP:
ALLOWED_HOSTS = ['YOUR_PUBLIC_IP']
ALLOWED_HOSTS = ['*']
'*' is fine for testing but not recommended for production.

Restart Gunicorn
After saving the file run:

sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
Then Open in Browser
http://YOUR_PUBLIC_IP

Your Django CRM site should load 

Why This Happens
When DEBUG = False, Django only allows requests from domains listed in ALLOWED_HOSTS.

So when you access using the EC2 public IP, Django blocks it and returns 400 Bad Request.
502 Bad Gateway
nginx/1.24.0 (Ubuntu)

Check Gunicorn Status
Run:
sudo systemctl status gunicorn

If it shows inactive or failed, restart it:
sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
Check Gunicorn Logs
journalctl -u gunicorn -n 50 --no-pager

Check if Socket File Exists
Your Nginx connects to Gunicorn using a socket.
Run:
ls /root/My_CRM/

Look for:
gunicorn.sock

If gunicorn.sock is missing, Gunicorn did not start properly.

Check Nginx Error Log (Most Important)
sudo tail -50 /var/log/nginx/error.log

connect() to unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock failed

Check Nginx Configuration
Open config:
sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/mycrm

Make sure it matches your Gunicorn socket:

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name YOUR_PUBLIC_IP;

    location / {
        include proxy_params;
        proxy_pass http://unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock;
    }

    location /static/ {
        root /root/My_CRM;
    }
}


Then test:
sudo nginx -t

Restart:
sudo systemctl restart nginx

Your project is located in:
/root/My_CRM

But Nginx runs as www-data user, which cannot access /root directory.

Run:
sudo chmod 755 /root
sudo chmod -R 755 /root/My_CRM

Then restart services:
sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
sudo systemctl restart nginx

Production apps are usually stored in:
/var/www/{permission essue}

sudo systemctl status gunicorn
and
sudo tail -20 /var/log/nginx/error.log

1️⃣ Change permissions of /root and project
Run:
sudo chmod 755 /root
sudo chmod -R 755 /root/My_CRM
2️⃣ Restart services
sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
sudo systemctl restart nginx
3️⃣ Test again

Modify your gunicorn.service so the socket has proper permissions.
Edit:
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/gunicorn.service
Add inside [Service]:
UMask=007

Reload:
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart gunicorn

